 - A patch that is not an array, or that has a hole in it, is rejected with a `JsonPatchError`.
 
+`createPatch(from, to)` produces the JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to`, so a client can send only what changed:
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+createPatch({ order: { items: ["A-1", "B-2"], note: "rush" } }, { order: { items: ["A-1", "B-2"], note: "gift" } });
+// [{ op: "replace", path: "/order/note", value: "gift" }]
+```
+
+- Objects are compared member by member and arrays element by element, so the patch describes the change and nothing
+  more. An edit deep in a large document gives one operation at its path.
+- Arrays are compared after the elements they share at the start and at the end are set aside. Inserting or removing one
+  element in the middle of an array gives one `add` or `remove`, not an operation for every element after it.
+- Equal documents give an empty patch. Object member order does not matter. A change of kind, such as an object becoming
+  an array, is a `replace` of the whole document at path `""`.
+- Member names are escaped in the pointers (`~` as `~0`, `/` as `~1`).
+- Neither input is mutated, and the values in the patch are copies, so the patch shares no structure with `from` or `to`.
+- `applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to))` gives a value equal to `to`.
+- `from` and `to` must be JSON values, as `applyPatch` requires of patch values; otherwise a `JsonPatchError` is thrown.
+
 ## JSON Merge Patch
 
 `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` applies a JSON Merge Patch ([RFC 7386](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386)), a partial
